<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Philaretos Brachamios</h1><p class="st-meta">1071 CE – 1086 CE (approx.) · capital Marash</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Armenian-ruled domain in the Taurus and northern Syria, built by a Byzantine general after the empire's collapse in the East.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos Brachamios</a> was an Armenian officer in Byzantine service. After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071 and the civil wars that followed, he gathered soldiers and Armenian refugees and made himself master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahramanmaras">Marash</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Melitene</a> and other towns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos</a> acted independently but sought recognition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which gave him high titles and appointed him governor in the East. At its height around 1078-1083 his domain stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">Edessa</a>. He was a Chalcedonian Christian, which made him unpopular with many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> of the national church, and he is said to have considered accepting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk</a> favour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-ibn-qutalmish">Suleiman ibn Qutalmish</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> in 1084, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-edessa">Edessa</a> fell soon after. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philaretos-brachamios">Philaretos</a> disappears from the record around 1086. Armenian lords of the region, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubenid-dynasty">Rubenids</a>, later formed the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1071 CE – 1086 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.01%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1071 CE – 1086 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marash</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philaretos Brachamios</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Domain of Philaretos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=principality-of-philaretos-brachamios" aria-label="Principality of Philaretos Brachamios on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5517 749 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Philaretos Brachamios lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5837 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5837 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkey. <a href="/atlas?year=1078&amp;focus=principality-of-philaretos-brachamios">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
